-- title:
--   $\psi$ convex along a family of subspaces (Definition 3)
-- statement:
--   Let $\psi:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$ and let $\mathcal U\subset\mathbb R^{n\times p}$ be a family of matrices, each generating the subspace spanned by its columns. The function $\psi$ is **convex along the family of subspaces** generated by $\mathcal U$ if for every fixed $x\in\mathbb R^n$ and every $U\in\mathcal U$ the restriction
--
--   $$
--   \phi_U^x(d)=\psi(x+Ud),\qquad d\in\mathbb R^p,
--   $$
--
--   is convex on $\mathbb R^p$.
--
--   Such a $\psi$ may be nonconvex on $\mathbb R^n$. The property selects the larger step size in Algorithm 1 and makes the subproblem (9) convex.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The domain of $\phi_U^x$ is all of $\mathbb R^p$ because $\psi$ is real valued.
-- source:
--   Necoara & Chorobura, Efficiency of stochastic coordinate proximal gradient methods on nonseparable composite optimization, arXiv:2104.13370v3, Definition 3, display (8), p.6

import Mathlib

namespace StochCoordProx

/-- **Convex along a family of subspaces** (Definition 3): `ψ : ℝⁿ → ℝ` is convex along the
family of subspaces generated by the matrices of `𝒰` if, for every fixed `x ∈ ℝⁿ` and every
`U ∈ 𝒰`, the restriction `φ_U^x(d) = ψ(x + U d)` of display (8) is convex on `ℝᵖ`.

Necoara & Chorobura, arXiv:2104.13370v3, Definition 3, display (8), p.6.

**Formalization Note.** The "corresponding domain" of `φ_U^x` is all of `ℝᵖ`, since `ψ` is
real valued on `ℝⁿ`; hence `ConvexOn ℝ Set.univ`. `U` acts as `Matrix.toEuclideanLin U`. -/
def ConvexAlongSubspaces {n p : ℕ} (ψ : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ)
    (𝒰 : Set (Matrix (Fin n) (Fin p) ℝ)) : Prop :=
  ∀ x : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), ∀ U ∈ 𝒰,
    ConvexOn ℝ Set.univ (fun d : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin p) => ψ (x + Matrix.toEuclideanLin U d))

end StochCoordProx


